                <pb facs="0955" n="641" sameAs="frus:frus1914Supp/pg641"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72111/864a</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to all Seaport Consuls
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <lb/>
                    <placeName>
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                        calendar="gregorian" when="1914-11-28">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">November 28, 1914</hi>.</date>
                </dateline>
                <p rendition="#center">[Circular telegram]</p>
                <p>Department’s circular instructions November 22<anchor n="1" xml:id="d1026-f1"/>
                    and 25. Reports teed not be made on vessels engaged especially in passenger
                    service but should be made on all American and foreign vessels arriving from
                    American ports in ballast or with cargo. You will not enforce investigation, nor
                    seek to detain vessels, nor do anything to offend authorities of port in
                    obtaining this information. You may discreetly obtain information from any
                    available source which need not be official. If you find that war vessels have
                    been supplied at sea, report their names if possible. Make reports brief.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
